KZ3 patch 2212 (RSX pass 176, revised after review): LD_WB_DEFER_4097=1 (default off) wires the "defer the 0x4A release" site of patch 2185 into the NV4097 0x1D70/0x1D74 handler, where KZ3 really releases label 0x4A, and holds the label store until the deferred bytes have landed.

Cause (read from the code and the profiling logs, NOT run in the game): patch 2185 set the deferral site only in the NV406E 0x6C block (cellGcmSys.c). KZ3 releases label 0x4A as NV4097 SET_SEMAPHORE_OFFSET(0x1D6C)=0x4A0 then 0x1D74, so the site never ran and the release drained the scene-copy fence at once (reason other=909 of 909 drains in profiling run g2).

Change: (1) rsx_commands.c brackets rsx_live_draw_before_guest_sync() of the 0x1D70/0x1D74 branch with rsx_live_draw_wbd_set_site_4097(label) / (-1). (2) Label hold (review finding: the label used to become visible to the PPU/SPU before the 4 main surfaces were stored): the three gcm_guest_signal(RSX_GS_SEM_BE) stores of that handler first ask rsx_live_draw_wbd_hold_label(); when the release deferred and stores are pending, the store (ea, val) is queued in rsx_wbd_hold (rsx_wb_defer.h) and published, in order, as soon as the pending-store queue is empty: after any ld_wbd_drain (user command 0xEB00, flip, slot, full, other), after a touch that empties the queue, and at the walker's end of pass (cellGcmSys.c, next to rsx_live_draw_submit_pending) so a label never waits for a command that is not in the ring yet. A PPU or SPU that waits for label 0x4A therefore sees it only after the bytes are in guest memory, as on hardware where the label follows the surface write. No PPU read hook is needed: the guest cannot order a read of these ranges after the release without the label. 16 held labels max; a 17th publishes the older ones first.

Counters: [wb-defer] 4097: releases_deferred, labels_held, labels_published and the drain reason that published them (held must equal published at run end).

Corrected estimate: this does NOT remove the walker's GPU wait. The wbdrain wait (scene-copy fence) moves from the 0x4A release to the 0xEB00 user command, which also waits for the 4 surface copies; the old second synchronous batch waited for the same GPU work. What is saved is the second wait's submit-to-wake latency and any walker work between the release and 0xEB00: upper bound flush_rb, 0.3-0.4 ms/flip in profiling runs g2 and p1, realistically 0.1-0.3 ms (about +0.1 fps, inside run noise). Treat this as a structural step: with the label hold the label store, the stores and the user command are one ordered completion that a later change can move to a worker so the walker records the next frame during the wait (that is where a real gain would be). Not default-on.

Required before any default-on: a run with LD_WB_DEFER_4097=1 LD_WB_DEFER_VERIFY=1 LD_WB_HASH=1 giving mismatches=0, spu_gets_of_pending=0, spu_writes_into_pending=0, labels_held=labels_published, and the main-surface ranges hash-equal at the 0xEB00 point against the switch-off baseline. Patch 2185 never deferred this label in KZ3 (the site was unwired), so the deferral has never run on real KZ3 data; the baseline counters of s123 (spu_*=0) were taken with only the NV0039 stores pending.

